import
	../../html

intro-exceptions html[]()
	- section "Throwing",
		- p quote
			{throw_} takes an {exception} and throws it.
			{br}
			{error} is a generic kind of exception used for program logic errors.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				throw error "boo"

		- p quote
			Since a {throw_} expression won't actually return normally, it can be any type.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				s string = throw error "boo"
				log s

	- section "Custom exception types",
		- p quote
			{exception} is a {variant_} type, so you can add new case types.
			{exception} has a single method {fn-show}.
			{br}
			(See {intro-link interfaces}.)
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				throw my-exception

			my-exception record exception case
			show string(_ my-exception) = "custom error message"

	- section "Assert and forbid",
		- p quote
			{assert_} throws an {error} if its argument is {function "false"}.
			{forbid_} throws an {error} if its argument is {function "true"}.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				assert 1 + 1 == 2
				forbid 2 * 2 == 4 # throws

		- p "An optional {else_} clause specifies the exception to throw."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				assert 1 + 1 == 3 else error "Not enough?"

		- p quote
			{assert_} and {forbid_} work with option conditions.
			{br}
			The option from an {assert_} will be available as a non-option after it.
			{br}
			The option from a {forbid_} will be available as a non-option in its {else_} clause.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				assert x = half 4
				log x
				# Will throw
				forbid y = half 4 else error "Did not expect \{y\}"

			half nat?(a nat)
				if is-even a then a / 2

		- p "{is_} is also a kind of assertion. Prefer this over {code "assert x == y"}."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				1 is 2 # fails

	- section "Finally",
		- p quote
			A {finally_} expression has {em "right"} and {em "below"} subexpressions.
			{br}
			If first runs the {em "below"} expression.
			{br}
			It then runs the {em "right"} expression, whether or not the {em "below"} expression threw an exception.
		- p quote
			Below, the {em "right"} expression is {fn-log}{sp}{string "\"right\""}
			and the {em "below"} expression is the sequence
			{fn-log}{sp}{string "\"below\""} and {log-1-over-0} .
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				finally log "right"
				log "below"
				log 1 / 0

		- aside-small quote
			Above, the division by 0 actually happens {em "before"} {fn-log}{sp}{string "\"right\""},
			but the exception is caught and rethrown after it runs.

		- p quote
			The value of a {finally_} expression is the value of the {em "below"} expression.
			The {em "right"} expression must be {void}.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				result =
					finally log "end"
					"value"
				log "result", result

		- p quote
			The {em "right"} expression can be an indented block.
			{br}
			Below, the {em "right"} expression is on lines 3 and 4
			and the {em "below"} expression is just {log-1-over-0} .
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				finally
					log "a"
					log "b"
				log 1 / 0

	- section "Catching exceptions",
		- p quote
			A {try_} expression first evaluates the {try_} block.
			If that threw the caught type, it evaluates the {catch_} block instead.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log try
					1 / 0
				catch as arithmetic-error
					7
		- aside-small quote
			The {try_} and {catch_} blocks must have the same type.

		- p "The {catch_} has no effect if some other kind of exception is thrown."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log try
					# Underflows 'nat'
					1 - 2
				catch as index-too-big
					7

		- p "A local name between {catch_} and {as_} will have the exception value."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log try
					to::nat8 256
				catch e as arithmetic-error
					log "\{nat64! only! e.inputs\} won't fit in a nat8"
					7

		- aside-small "See {arithmetic-error} for details."

		- p "There can be multiple {catch_} blocks for different exception types."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				xs nat[] = ()
				log try
					xs[1 / 0]
				catch as arithmetic-error
					7
				catch as index-too-big
					8

		- p "If there is no {as_} clause, it catches every exception."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log try
					1 / 0
				catch e
					log "Something happened: \{e\}"
					7
		- aside-small quote
			You should almost always include an {as_} clause.
			You don't want to ignore an out of memory error
			based on an assumption that {arithmetic-error} was the only possible exception.

	- section "Common exceptions",
		- p quote
			Throw {std-function "keen/exception", "not-implemented"} if something is intentionally not implemented.
			{br}
			Throw {std-function "keen/exception", "todo"} if something hasn't been implemented, but should be.
			{br}
			Throw {std-function "keen/exception", "unreachable"}
			if it should not be possible to get to the {throw_} expression.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log describe 1
				log describe 2

			describe string(a nat)
				match a % 2
				as 0
					"even"
				as 1
					"odd"
				else
					throw unreachable

	- section "Testing exceptions",
		- p "Use this pattern to test functions that should throw exceptions:"
		- keen-testable quote
			test
				try
					_ = to::string 1::json
					should-throw
				catch e as deserialize-error
					e is expected: "string", value: 1
		- aside-small quote
			Tests will be explained in {intro-link tests} .
			{code "<<no output>>"} means the test passed.

	- section "\"is\"",
		- p quote
			{is_} parses as a binary operator with the lowest precedence.
			{br}
			It asserts that the left side is equal to the right side.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				round 1.5 is 2
				# is equivalent to:
				assert (round 1.5) == 2

		- p "Like {assert_}, {is_} can throw a custom exception with an {else_} clause."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				1 is 2 else error "custom exception"

		- p "There's also {isnt_}."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				1 isnt 2

-arithmetic-error html-node()
	std-type "keen/number", "arithmetic-error"
-error html-node()
	std-type "keen/exception", "error"

-log-1-over-0 html[]()
	"{fn-log}{sp}{num-1}{sp}{function "/"}{sp}{num-0}"
